\section{An Actor Chooses an Action, Not Merely an Operation}

CAL describes actors with typed ports, private state, and actions. An action is enabled only when its input pattern can be satisfied, its guard is true, its finite-state-machine transition permits it, and no enabled higher-priority action excludes it. The network supplies FIFO communication; it does not turn the body of an actor into a stateless function. Brunet's account of dynamic dataflow stresses precisely these input-pattern, guard, state, and priority dimensions. They are semantic inputs to compilation, not profiling annotations.

This representative actor routes nonnegative and negative tokens to separate ports:

\begin{verbatim}
actor Split() int I ==> int P, int N:
  pos: action I:[v] ==> P:[v]
       guard v >= 0 end
  neg: action I:[v] ==> N:[v]
       guard v < 0 end
end
\end{verbatim}

Both actions inspect the same head token and consume one token if selected. Their guards are disjoint, so the result is independent of which enabled action is tested first. In a general actor, overlapping guards can make priority or scheduler choice observable. A compiler must not replace ``select one enabled action'' with ``run every enabled action.'' CAL's actor model is therefore more dynamic than synchronous dataflow with fixed rates \cite{lee1987sdf}, although restricted actors may admit a static schedule.

\section{Keep Readiness, Selection, and Commit Distinct}

A useful actor IR has actor instances, FIFO edges, and an ordered action table. Each action records peeked and consumed token counts by port, produced counts, a pure guard region, state reads, a body region, state writes, and successor FSM state. Priority is a relation over action IDs, not source order accidentally retained in a list. This representation makes a readiness query different from an atomic firing. \Cref{fig:visual-367} shows the three phases for the example.

