The implementation may lower each actor to a dispatch loop, generate a runtime actor object, or specialize a statically schedulable subnetwork. Its output artifacts should include executable code, FIFO descriptors, an action-selection table, and machine-readable rate and effect summaries. An execution-trace graph is a useful profiling artifact, but a trace contains observed firings rather than all legal firings.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Lower One Atomic Firing and Check Its Contract}

\begin{verbatim}
heads := peek_required_inputs(actor)        # no consumption yet
candidates := actions_matching_fsm_and_patterns(heads)
enabled := [a for a in candidates if eval_guard(a, heads, state)]
chosen := maximal_under_priority(enabled)
if chosen exists:
    values, next_state := run_body_in_private_staging(chosen)
    atomically consume inputs, publish values, and install next_state
else:
    suspend actor until relevant input or state can change
\end{verbatim}

Guard common-subexpression elimination is legal when the expression is pure and reads the same peeked tokens and state snapshot. An action may be specialized for a known FSM state, and mutually exclusive actions may share tests. Action fusion, reordering, or speculative consumption is not legal merely because it is faster: it must preserve token order, priority, blocking behavior, and atomic state/output visibility. A guard that can trap or call an effectful routine needs an explicit effect contract before motion.

Validation begins with structural checks that every referenced port, state slot, priority edge, and FSM transition exists. Differential tests run the source actor interpreter and lowered artifact on the same finite input queues, comparing consumed prefixes, output sequences, final state, and whether the actor blocks. For bounded models, selection can also be explored over all head tokens and states. These checks preserve the actor's observable firing relation; they do not claim that an arbitrary network has a unique schedule or deterministic result.

\section{Implement a Priority Test That Consumes Nothing}

Suppose a different actor handles one input port with two possible actions: \texttt{zero} routes the value \(0\) to a special output, and \texttt{fallback} routes any value to a general output. Give \texttt{zero} priority over \texttt{fallback} when both are enabled. The empty queue satisfies neither pattern; the fallback has an unrestricted guard, not an unrestricted input pattern.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Head & Guard-enabled set & Selection \\
\hline
absent & none & wait on input \\
0 & zero, fallback & zero \\
7 & fallback & fallback \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
fire_example(input, outputs):
    snapshot := peek_one(input)
    if snapshot missing: return NEED_INPUT
    if snapshot.value == 0: choice := zero
    else: choice := fallback
    if not reserve_output(outputs[choice]):
        return NEED_OUTPUT_SPACE
    consume_exactly_one(input)
    publish_reserved(outputs[choice], snapshot.value)
    return FIRED
\end{verbatim}

For this example choose a bounded runtime that selects as if the output channels were unbounded and stalls only at commit. If the special output is full and the head is zero, do not fire fallback to make progress: that would change which output contains the zero. The trial must leave both the head and actor state intact until the reservation becomes available. Verify this with the input \([0,7]\), a full special queue and an empty general queue. First expect no consumption, then drain the special queue and expect one item on each output after two firings. Backends with different backpressure contracts need to document whether capacity participates in selection.
